---
import Simple from '../layouts/Simple.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import { faqNode } from '../lib/schema';

const path = '/pricing';
const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Pricing', path },
];
const rows = [
  { s: 'First consultation', w: 'History, exam and a plan for which labs you need', p: '[[CONFIRM: consultation fee]]', l: '/assessment' },
  { s: 'Lab panels', w: 'Blood draw, the panel and a results review with a clinician', p: '[[CONFIRM: panels and prices]]', l: '/lab-testing' },
  { s: 'Testosterone therapy', w: 'Visits, medication and monitoring labs', p: '[[CONFIRM: TRT program price and what it includes]]', l: '/treatments/testosterone-therapy' },
  { s: 'Hormone therapy for women', w: 'Visits, prescriptions and follow-up', p: "[[CONFIRM: women's hormone therapy program price and what it includes]]", l: '/treatments/hormone-therapy-for-women' },
  { s: 'Medical weight loss', w: 'Visits, labs and medication where prescribed', p: '[[CONFIRM: medical weight loss program price and what it includes]]', l: '/treatments/medical-weight-loss' },
  { s: 'Sexual health', w: 'Evaluation, labs and treatment plan', p: '[[CONFIRM: sexual health visit price and what it includes]]', l: '/treatments/sexual-health' },
  { s: 'IV therapy', w: 'Per drip, in the clinic', p: '[[CONFIRM: drip menu and prices]]', l: '/treatments/iv-therapy' },
  { s: 'Mobile IV travel fee', w: 'Added to the drip price when we come to you', p: '[[CONFIRM: mobile service area and fee]]', l: '/mobile-iv/hallandale-beach' },
  { s: 'Follow-up visit', w: 'In person or by video', p: '[[CONFIRM: follow-up visit fee]]', l: '/telehealth' },
];
const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Does insurance cover TRT?',
    a: 'Sometimes. Insurers that cover testosterone usually want two low morning blood tests, symptoms and a qualifying diagnosis on record, and many require prior approval. Compounded testosterone and pellets are rarely covered. Whether this clinic bills insurance at all: [[CONFIRM: insurance and payment policy]]',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I pay with an HSA or FSA card?',
    a: 'Under IRS rules, doctor visits, lab fees and prescription medication generally count as qualified medical expenses for HSA and FSA funds. IV therapy for general wellness may not. [[CONFIRM: whether HSA and FSA cards are accepted]]',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a contract or membership?',
    a: '[[CONFIRM: membership or pay-per-visit model, minimum term and cancellation policy]]',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the cheapest way to find out if I have a hormone problem?',
    a: 'A lab panel. It costs far less than a treatment program, the results are yours to keep, and there is no obligation to start anything. See [lab testing](/lab-testing).',
  },
];
---

<Simple
  path={path}
  title="Hormone Therapy Cost in Florida | Hormonal Health MD"
  description="What hormone therapy, TRT, weight loss care and IV drips cost, what drives the price and whether insurance or HSA funds apply. Know before you commit."
  eyebrow="Hormone therapy cost in Florida"
  headline="Know what you will pay, *before you commit.*"
  lede="Hormone care has three costs: lab work, clinician visits and medication. Here is how each works at our clinic, and the questions worth asking any clinic before you sign anything."
  updated="2026-11-09"
  crumbs={crumbs}
  schema={[faqNode(path, faqs)]}
>
  <section class="section section--paper">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Price list</p>
        <h2>What each service <em>costs.</em></h2>
        <p>Prices are confirmed by the clinic before this page goes live. Nothing here is an estimate.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="ptable">
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Service</th><th scope="col">What it covers</th><th scope="col">Price</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              rows.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <td><a href={r.l}>{r.s}</a></td>
                  <td>{r.w}</td>
                  <td>{r.p}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap--narrow prose">
      <h2>How much does hormone therapy cost in Florida?</h2>
      <p>
        It depends on three things: the lab work you need, how often you are seen and which
        medication you are prescribed. Two people on hormone therapy can pay very different
        amounts for good reasons, which is why a single headline price rarely tells you much.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our own prices for each part are in the table above. [[CONFIRM: typical all-in monthly
        cost range for a patient on TRT and for a patient on menopause hormone therapy]]
      </p>

      <h2>What makes TRT cost more or less?</h2>
      <p>
        The form of testosterone is the biggest factor. Generic injections cost the least.
        Brand-name gels and pellets cost more, and pellets add a procedure fee each time they
        are placed. Monitoring is the second factor: guidelines call for labs three to six
        months after starting and at least yearly after that.
      </p>
      <p>
        See <a href="/treatments/testosterone-therapy">testosterone therapy</a> for how the forms
        compare.
      </p>

      <h2>Does insurance cover TRT or hormone therapy?</h2>
      <p>
        Coverage varies by plan. Insurers that cover testosterone typically require two low
        morning blood tests, documented symptoms and prior approval. FDA-approved generic
        estradiol and progesterone for menopause are commonly covered and inexpensive at a
        pharmacy. Compounded hormones and pellets usually are not covered.
      </p>
      <p>
        Weight loss medication is the least predictable. Many plans exclude it, and the rules
        have been changing, so check your own plan. [[CONFIRM: insurance and payment policy]]
      </p>

      <h2>Can I use my HSA or FSA?</h2>
      <p>
        Usually, for the medical parts. The IRS treats doctor visits, lab fees and prescription
        medication as qualified medical expenses. Treatments for general wellness may not
        qualify. [[CONFIRM: whether HSA and FSA cards are accepted]]
      </p>

      <h2>What should I ask any clinic about price?</h2>
      <p>
        Ask the same six questions everywhere you look, including here. A clinic that answers
        them plainly is usually a clinic that treats you plainly.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Is lab work included, or billed separately each time?</li>
        <li>Is the medication included in the price, and which form is it?</li>
        <li>How often will I need follow-up labs, and what do they cost?</li>
        <li>Is there a contract, a minimum term or a cancellation fee?</li>
        <li>Who reviews my labs, and what are their credentials?</li>
        <li>What happens, and what do I pay, if my labs come back normal?</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Is there a low-cost way to start?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes. Start with a blood panel. It is the least expensive step, it answers the question
        of whether a hormone problem exists at all, and you keep the results whatever you
        decide. See <a href="/lab-testing">lab testing</a>, or take the
        <a href="/assessment">assessment</a> to see which panel fits. You can read about who
        reviews your results on the <a href="/team">team page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--sand">
    <div class="wrap--narrow">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Questions</p>
        <h2>Paying for <em>care.</em></h2>
      </div>
      <Faq faqs={faqs} />
      <p class="small muted" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <Icon name="doc" class="inline-i" />
        Source for HSA and FSA rules:
        <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p502" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">IRS Publication 502, Medical and Dental Expenses</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Simple>

<style>
  :global(.inline-i) {
    display: inline;
    width: 1rem;
    height: 1rem;
    vertical-align: -0.15em;
  }
</style>
